{"reference":"Titus 2","book":"Titus","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter distributes ethical instruction among older men, older women, younger women, younger men, Titus himself, and enslaved people. Its account of “sound doctrine” is therefore not limited to assent to teaching: it is portrayed in conduct. Verses 11–14 then present grace as having appeared for salvation and as training people for self-controlled, just, and godly living while they await hope.","verse_refs":["Titus 2:1-10","Titus 2:11-13","Titus 2:4-5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_002_44ef2d069c6c7176bb42213c"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Household Instruction and Teaching","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The instructions to younger women reflect ancient household reputation and labor conventions; they are not, by themselves, a complete timeless definition of women’s vocation. At the same time, older women are explicitly told to teach what is good and train younger women. These details matter when interpreting a passage whose household language has often been extended beyond what it directly says.","verse_refs":["Titus 2:1-10","Titus 2:1-8","Titus 2:3-5","Titus 2:6-8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_002_5b9c5c3671d196b9609f6664"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Titus as an Example","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Titus is not only told what to say. He is to make his own conduct an example of good works, integrity, dignity, and sound speech. The passage thus holds his teaching and his public manner together.","verse_refs":["Titus 2:6-8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_002_19fa5aae0a034635aab7d573"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Enslaved People and Grace","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Verses 9–10 address enslaved people within an exploitative Roman institution, directing them toward submission and trustworthiness without commanding enslavers to abolish slavery. That limitation must be kept visible: the passage’s language cannot justify slavery, trafficking, worker exploitation, concealment of abuse, or demands for compliance from people in danger.","verse_refs":["Titus 2:11-13","Titus 2:14","Titus 2:9-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_002_b925d82f03956fa384fbbcca"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"The chapter personifies grace as appearing and training people to reject destructive desire and live justly while awaiting hope. Its “appearing” language joins salvation’s manifestation with expectation of the glory of “the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ.” The precise grammatical relationship in verse 13 has received detailed discussion, so the wording should not be made to settle questions beyond the passage without care. Verse 14 connects Christ’s self-giving with redemption, purification, and a people zealous for good works; this does not glorify abuse, coerced sacrifice, or exploitation.","verse_refs":["Titus 2:11-13","Titus 2:14","Titus 2:9-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_002_b925d82f03956fa384fbbcca"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"things_easy_to_miss","title":"Good Works and a Treasured People","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Good works recur as the public fruit of grace, rather than as the basis for earning divine mercy. The description of a people as Christ’s own or treasured people echoes scriptural peoplehood language, but it should not be turned into ethnic superiority, nationalism, or replacement of another people.","verse_refs":["Titus 2:1-10","Titus 2:11-14","Titus 2:14","Titus 2:3-8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_002_c82a96a3278855e25d8bba45"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"A Manuscript Witness","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"Papyrus 32 preserves parts of Titus 1:11–15 and Titus 2:3–8 and is commonly dated around 200 CE. It is therefore a partial witness to the chapter’s instruction about older and younger women, younger men, and Titus’s example, not to every verse in Titus 2.","verse_refs":["Titus 2:14","Titus 2:3-5","Titus 2:3-8","Titus 2:9-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_002_fdb06318fc6fb176100b2512"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Titus Responsibly","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"When drawing contemporary conclusions from Titus as a whole, it is important to distinguish the biblical wording, the letter’s voice, historical reconstruction, lexical claims, doctrine, later reception, pastoral judgment, and modern analogy. Keeping those levels distinct helps prevent a conclusion drawn from one level from being presented as though the text itself stated it directly.","verse_refs":["Titus 1:1-16","Titus 3:1-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_002_84cbff9104a73455fb87a6bc"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_9","text":"The surviving Codex Vaticanus ends before the Pastoral Epistles, so it is not a manuscript witness to Titus.","verse_refs":["Titus 1:1-16","Titus 3:1-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_titus_002_42daa9391a194891c78cc267"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}